subcode:: sections
    $call section, started, Getting Started
    $p
        This chapter walks through creating your first $(code:MyDef) project step by step. By the end, you will understand the basic workflow and core concepts. We assume you have already installed $(code:MyDef) following the instructions in the $(a:Installations,#install) chapter.

    $call subsection, started_general, Hello World with $(code:output_general)
    $p
        Let's start with the simplest possible example. Create a file called $(code:hello.def) with the following content:
        &call codeprint, mydef, hello.def
            page: hello
                Hello, World!
    $p
        This defines a single $(code:page) named $(code:hello). The indented line beneath it is the page's content. Now compile it:
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_page hello.def
            PAGE: hello
              --> [./hello.txt]
    $p
        $(code:mydef_page) read the $(code:.def) file, processed the page, and wrote the output to $(code:hello.txt). Since we did not specify a module, $(code:output_general) was used by default, which produces $(code:.txt) files. The output file contains exactly what we wrote:
        &call codeprint
            Hello, World!
    $p
        That is the core of $(code:MyDef): you write $(code:.def) files and compile them into output files. With $(code:output_general), the output is plain text with macros expanded and subcodes inlined. Other output modules add language-specific features on top.

    $call subsection, started_perl, Hello World with $(code:output_perl)
    $p
        Now let's try generating a Perl script. Create $(code:hello_perl.def):
        &call codeprint, mydef, hello_perl.def
            page: hello_perl
                module: perl
                $print Hello, World!
    $p
        The $(code:module: perl) attribute tells $(code:MyDef) to use $(code:output_perl). The $(code:$print) syntax is a convenience provided by $(code:output_perl) -- it generates a $(code:print) statement with automatic quoting and newline.
    $p
        Instead of compiling and running separately, we can use $(code:mydef_run) to do both in one step:
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_run hello_perl.def
            PAGE: hello_perl
              --> [./hello_perl.pl]
            perl ./hello_perl.pl
            Hello, World!
    $p
        Let's look at the generated $(code:hello_perl.pl):
        &call codeprint, perl
            &num;!/usr/bin/perl
            use strict;

            print "Hello, World!\n";
    $p
        $(code:output_perl) automatically added the $(code:#!) shebang line, $(code:use strict;), quotation marks, $(code:\n), and the semicolon. You wrote one line; $(code:MyDef) produced correct, complete Perl.

    $call subsection, started_subcode, First Subcode
    $p
        A $(code:subcode) is a reusable block of code. It is the primary abstraction in $(code:MyDef). Here is an example:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            page: greet
                module: perl
                $call greet, Alice
                $call greet, Bob
                $call greet, Carol

            subcode: greet(name)
                $print Hello, $(name)!
    $p
        $(code:subcode: greet(name)) defines a block with one parameter (see $(a:Subcode with parameters,#subcode_param)). $(code:$call greet, Alice) inlines that block with $(code:&dollar;(name)) replaced by $(code:Alice). Running this produces:
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_run greet.def
            PAGE: greet
              --> [./greet.pl]
            perl ./greet.pl
            Hello, Alice!
            Hello, Bob!
            Hello, Carol!
    $p
        The generated Perl looks like this:
        &call codeprint, perl
            &num;!/usr/bin/perl
            use strict;

            print "Hello, Alice!\n";
            print "Hello, Bob!\n";
            print "Hello, Carol!\n";
    $p
        The subcode was expanded three times, each time with a different parameter value. This is pure text substitution -- there is no function call overhead at runtime.
    $p
        When you need to call a subcode many times with different single parameters, $(code:$map) provides a shorthand:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $map greet, Alice, Bob, Carol
    $p
        This produces the same output as the three separate $(code:$call) lines above.

    $call subsection, started_block, First Block Call
    $p
        Many code patterns have an opening part and a closing part with varying content in between. $(code:MyDef) handles this with $(code:&call) (block call) and the $(code:BLOCK) keyword. Here is an example:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            page: wrap
                module: perl
                &call open_w, output.txt
                    $(set:print_to=Out)
                    $print Line 1
                    $print Line 2
    $p
        $(code:open_w) is a subcode from $(code:MyDef)'s standard library. It opens a file for writing. The indented block beneath $(code:&call) replaces the $(code:BLOCK) keyword inside the subcode definition.
    $p
        To understand the mechanism, here is a simplified version of what $(code:open_w) looks like:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: open_w(file)
                open Out, ">$(file)" or die "Can't open $(file)\n"
                BLOCK
                close Out
    $p
        When you write $(code:&call open_w, output.txt), $(code:MyDef) expands it as:
        &call codeprint, perl
            open Out, ">output.txt" or die "Can't open output.txt\n";
            print Out "Line 1\n";
            print Out "Line 2\n";
            close Out;
    $p
        The key difference between $(code:$call) and $(code:&call):
    $ul
        $li
            $(code:$call) -- inline expansion. No indented block follows.
        $li
            $(code:&call) -- block expansion. An indented block follows and replaces $(code:BLOCK) inside the subcode.

    $call subsection, started_macro, First Macro
    $p
        Macros are compile-time text substitutions. They are defined in a $(code:macros:) block and referenced with $(code:&dollar;(name)) syntax:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            macros:
                greeting: Hello
                audience: World

            page: macro_demo
                module: perl
                $print $(greeting), $(audience)!
    $p
        This generates:
        &call codeprint, perl
            print "Hello, World!\n";
    $p
        Macros are expanded before any language processing. They work the same regardless of the output module.
    $p
        For macros that are only needed locally, use $(code:&dollar;(set:...)) instead of a $(code:macros:) block:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: matrix_multiply
                $(set:N=4)
                $(set:m=mat[$1][$2])
                $for i=0:$(N)
                    $for j=0:$(N)
                        $(m:C,i) += $(m:A,i) * $(m:B,j)
    $p
        $(code:&dollar;(set:...)) macros expire at the end of their enclosing scope, keeping the namespace clean.

    $call subsection, started_workflow, Project Workflow
    $p
        For a single-file program, $(code:mydef_run) is all you need. For larger projects with multiple output files, the typical workflow uses $(code:mydef_make) to generate a $(code:Makefile).
    $p
        Start by creating a project directory with a $(code:config) file:
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mkdir myproject
            $ cd myproject
    $p
        Create a $(code:config) file specifying where output goes and which module to use:
        &call codeprint, mydef, config
            output_dir: out
            module: perl
    $p
        Create your $(code:.def) source files, then run $(code:mydef_make):
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_make
                output_dir: out
            Create output folder out ...
    $p
        This scans all $(code:.def) files in the directory, determines their dependencies, and generates a $(code:Makefile). Now you can use the standard $(code:make) workflow:
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ make
    $p
        After editing $(code:.def) files, simply run $(code:make) again -- it will only recompile files whose dependencies have changed.
    $p
        The typical development cycle is:
    $ul
        $li
            For quick experiments: edit $(code:.def), run $(code:mydef_run file.def)
        $li
            For projects: edit $(code:.def), run $(code:make) (after initial $(code:mydef_make))
        $li
            To install: run $(code:make install) (if an $(code:install_def.sh) is set up)

    $call subsection, started_summary, Summary of Concepts
    $p
        Here is a summary of the core concepts introduced in this chapter:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Concept"
            $th "Definition"
            $th "Usage"
        $tr
            $td "Page"
            $td
                $(code:page: name)
            $td "Compiled automatically into an output file"
        $tr
            $td "Subcode"
            $td
                $(code:subcode: name(params))
            $td
                $(code:$call name, args)
        $tr
            $td "Block call"
            $td
                Subcode with $(code:BLOCK) keyword
            $td
                $(code:&call name, args) + indented block
        $tr
            $td "Macro"
            $td
                $(code:macros:) block
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(name))
        $tr
            $td "Dynamic macro"
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(set:name=value))
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(name)), expires at end of scope
        $tr
            $td "Module"
            $td
                $(code:module: perl) under page
            $td "Selects the output module"
    $p
        With these basics, you can already start writing useful programs. The following chapters cover the $(a:full syntax,#syntax) and language-specific features in detail&#44; and the $(a:Reference,#reference) chapter collects them in tables.
